23:30:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: I understand you’re anticipating a specific dynamic, and it’s smart to plan ahead. Instead of leaning into the stereotype (which can create a self-fulfilling prophecy), I’d focus on managing *any* group where a few people tend to dominate with lengthy personal anecdotes. Here’s a practical, respectful way to keep meetings productive without anyone feeling singled out. ### 1. Reset the meeting culture from the start In your first meeting with this group, calmly set shared norms. Frame them as a way to respect everyone’s time and make sure all voices are heard. For example: > “To make our meetings efficient, I’ll ask that we stick to a tight agenda. Updates should be brief and directly relevant—save the broader storytelling for one-on-ones or our team social time. I’ll be a stickler for the clock so we get out on time.” This makes the expectation universal, not aimed at any one person. ### 2. Use a tightly timed agenda with specific slots Don’t leave open space for tangents. Distribute an agenda ahead of time with timeboxes. For each item, note: - Topic - Owner - Allotted minutes - Outcome needed (decision, info-sharing, brainstorm) Example: - Project status round (2 min each, no discussion) – 10 min - Decision on vendor (10 min) - Roadblock removal (5 min per person, flag in advance) Enforce the clock publicly: “We have 2 minutes left on this, so let’s land on next steps.” If a story begins, you can say: “Hold that thought—let’s take it offline. I want to make sure we cover the rest of the agenda.” ### 3. Structure updates as written pre-reads, not spoken monologues If status updates are where the long-windedness creeps in, shift to a written format. Use a shared doc or Slack thread where everyone posts their update before the meeting. In the meeting, only surface issues or questions that need group discussion. That starves anecdote-laden retellings of oxygen. ### 4. Deploy strategic facilitation techniques - **Go round-robin for brief input** but limit it: “One sentence on the biggest risk you see—no backstory, just the headline.” - **Use a “parking lot”** (a visible space—whiteboard or shared note) for off-topic ideas or stories. When someone goes long, say: “That sounds interesting—let’s park it and you can give me the full version later.” - **Leverage non-verbal cues:** A gentle hand raise can signal “time to wrap up” without interrupting. If you have a co-facilitator, they can help monitor air-time. - **Designate a timekeeper** (rotate the role) who gives a one-minute warning and a hard stop. ### 5. Address the storytelling directly if it persists If someone consistently derails the meeting, have a kind, private conversation. Focus on impact, not the Ivy League caricature: > “I’ve noticed you have a lot of great context to share, but in our tight meetings, we need to condense updates to the core points so we can finish on time. Could you try to distill your point to two sentences in the meeting, and if it needs more background, send it in an email or we can chat separately?” Most people appreciate clear boundaries when they’re framed as helping the team. ### 6. Model brevity yourself Keep your own stories short. When you must give context, do it in one preview sentence and offer to provide details later. If the leader rambles, it gives everyone else permission. ### A note on the stereotype You’ve been told to expect long-winded stories “the way they are,” but I’d caution against leading with that assumption. People rise or fall to the expectations we set. By establishing crisp, inclusive meeting habits from day one, you may be pleasantly surprised—and you’ll avoid the trap of managing a caricature rather than actual individuals. With clear structure and consistent facilitation, you’ll run meetings that are efficient, respectful, and focused on outcomes, regardless of alma maters.
